Z||000000||SLYGUY||09-15-2001||06:57 PM||||I had the brakes checked and asked the service guy to take a look at the front suspension to see if it had been modified because I get a little bounce occasionally when driving it.<P>The service rep told me to take a look. Sure enough, the previous owner (I'm the 2nd) had heated the springs and you can tell!! <BR>The bottom rings are touching and the whole thing looks a little sickly.<BR>Gotta replace those now. <BR>Any ideas as where they would be the cheapest.<P>P.S. I'm not in a hurry though because every other fiero I see looks like it has been raised or something compared to how sleek mine looks.<P>Sly<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/SLYGUYSIG.jpg"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Leadfoot||09-15-2001||06:58 PM||||Try tire rack.  They have pretty inexpensive springs.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Rick 88||09-15-2001||07:19 PM||||Sly,<P>Stock 88's tend to sit nose high. Fiero Store sells new front lowering springs separatly if you do not want to change the rears. The other option is to find a set of coupe springs and remove 1 coil. This will give you a slight nose down attitude. Not nearly as low as you are now. The spring rate will increase but you are starting with a softer spring than on the GT. I have this set up with 17 in wheels and my ride is very good.<P>Hope this helps,<P>Rick <BR>yellow 88 GT 5-speed||||reg||1
